[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019] STEP PURPOSE
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019] --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   TEP / Planet-9 -- step b4: geometry of the detached orbit poles
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   Three pictures predict different three-dimensional pole
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   structure for the detached population:
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   * point-mass shepherding: orbits share a common plane tilted
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   toward the perturber -- poles cluster about ONE direction
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   (a point on the pole sphere).
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   * inclination instability / self-gravity (Madigan & McCourt):
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   the disk torques itself into a warped plane -- poles spread
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   along a GREAT CIRCLE (a girdle), not a point.
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   * fixed field boundary (TEP): if the structure is a lapse
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   gradient it selects perihelion direction; if it also tilts
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   the clock planes it leaves a preferred pole direction --
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   either way, the eigenstructure of the pole scatter matrix
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   discriminates point (l1 >> l2 ~ l3) from girdle
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   (l1 ~ l2 >> l3).
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   Because low-i orbits trivially place poles near the ecliptic pole,
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   everything is calibrated against the conditioned null (fix each
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   object's i, draw om uniform) -- the null ring of poles at radius
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   sin(i) is itself girdle-like, so the observed statistics are
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   compared to that null, not to isotropy.
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   Statistics: scatter-matrix eigenvalues l1>=l2>=l3 of the folded
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   poles; cluster index C = l1 - l2 (single-direction excess);
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   girdle index G = l2 - l3 (planar excess).  The same decomposition
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   is applied to the (unfolded) perihelion vectors.
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   Also reported: the best common-plane normal (l3 eigenvector of
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   the girdle fit / direction that minimises pole spread), its
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   ecliptic coordinates and tilt, and its relation to the varpi
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   cluster axis -- whether the pole structure and the perihelion
[2026-09-20 10:42:19 UTC] [INFO    ] [STEP 019]   structure point at the same inertial frame.
